text:
alex: Heads up — running into sporadic KMS Decrypt failures from our staging pods when we roll secrets. Suspect STS session name length + rotation causing key policy condition failures. Anyone seen this?

jess: Which pods? IRSA with sessionName set via k8s annotation (aws-sdk-default) or do you pass AssumeRole session name explicitly?

alex: Old operator sets session name to `${ns}-${sa}-${rand(6)}` via terraform template. When we rotate keys, CloudTrail shows AssumedRole sessionName longer than 64 chars and KMS key policy uses StringLike on aws:PrincipalTag/sessionName so it fails. Example principal in logs: `arn:aws:sts::123456:assumed-role/infra-role/sessionname-with-extra-chars`

matt: Oof. KMS condition usually checks aws:PrincipalTag/SessionName? We typically use aws:RequestTag/sessionName in policy for tagging path. Could you paste TF snippet?

alex: Sure, here's what we have in TF:```hcl
resource "kubernetes_service_account" "svc" {
  metadata {
    name = "secret-updater"
    namespace = var.env_ns
    annotations = {
      "eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn" = aws_iam_role.irsa.arn
      "eks.amazonaws.com/sts-session-name" = "${var.env_ns}-${local.svc_name}-${random_string.suffix.result}"
    }
  }
}
```

jess: I think the annotation key is nonstandard — EKS IRSA doesn't have a built-in sts-session-name annotation. We're using controller that passes it to STS. Which controller?

alex: Using our internal webhook + kube2iam-like helper. It sets sessionName and tags role session with SessionName tag. But TF random_string can produce long values (we had long ns in preprod).

matt: Short term: truncate sessionName in template to 32 chars. Long term: update KMS policy to allow prefix via StringLike and check aws:PrincipalArn maybe. Also consider using kms:EncryptionContext instead of principal tags.

jess: Can you try replacing annotation with `${substr(var.env_ns,0,8)}-${substr(local.svc_name,0,8)}-${random_string.suffix.result}` and redeploy to see if decrypts stop failing?

alex: Will try. Also curious about rotation timing: when we rotate key with re-encrypt, do existing session tags on assumed role stop matching? CloudTrail shows role still assumed but tag absent during re-encrypt window.

matt: IAM session tags are ephemeral for that STS session; re-encrypt shouldn't affect existing sessions, but if clients re-assume role mid-rotation and session name/generation mismatches policy will deny. Ensure key policy allows access via role ARN fallback: Condition Null check on aws:PrincipalTag/sessionName -> allow if role ARN matches.

jess: If you need a quick policy patch, here's a snippet that allows role ARN or the session tag prefix:
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456:role/infra-role"},
      "Action": ["kms:Decrypt","kms:Encrypt","kms:ReEncrypt*"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ForAnyValue:StringLike": {
          "aws:PrincipalArn": "arn:aws:iam::123456:role/infra-role",
          "aws:PrincipalTag/sessionName": "${var.env_ns}-${local.svc_name}*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

alex: Thanks, patching to test. Will report back in ~30m. Also filing an infra ticket: INFRA-3421 to track making sessionName deterministic and adding len guard.

matt: :thumbsup: Also add a unit in terraform to validate length and print warning.

alex: Done. Tests passed on staging, no decrypt errors after truncation. Leaving ticket for follow-up on policy change.

infra-bot: Created ticket INFRA-3421 for "sessionName length hardening + KMS policy fallback" assigned to @alex and @matt with priority P2.
